<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=diesel-engine">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Energy and power</p><h1 class="enc-title">Diesel engine</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1893">1893</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as compression-ignition engine</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A diesel engine is an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/internal-combustion-engine">internal combustion engine</a> in which the fuel is ignited by the heat of air compressed in the cylinder, without a spark plug. The German engineer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-diesel">Rudolf Diesel</a> published his idea in 1893 in Theory and Construction of a Rational Heat Motor, and his first prototype was built that summer at the Maschinenfabrik <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a>, with the first ignition on 10 August 1893. A redesigned engine ran for 88 revolutions in February 1894, and the third prototype, tested on 17 February 1897, reached an effective efficiency of 26.2%, against the 6–10% of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steam-engine">steam engines</a> that Diesel had learned of as a student.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diesel-engine.jpg" alt="Diesel engine" width="330" height="314" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: High Contrast · CC BY 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MAN_TGX_V8_engine.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In 1878 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-diesel">Rudolf Diesel</a>, then a student at the Polytechnikum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, attended the lectures of Carl von Linde, who explained that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steam-engine">steam engines</a> convert just 6 to 10 percent of heat energy into work, whereas the Carnot cycle allows much more. According to Diesel this gave him the idea of a highly efficient engine working on that cycle. He was also shown a fire piston, a traditional fire starter based on rapid compression.</p><p>Diesel sought firms to build the engine and, with help from Moritz Schröter and Max Gutermuth, persuaded both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krupp">Krupp</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essen">Essen</a> and the Maschinenfabrik Augsburg. He obtained a patent on 23 February 1893, signed a contract with Krupp on 10 April, and on 24 April both firms agreed to build a single prototype at Augsburg. In the winter of 1893 and 1894 he redesigned the engine and added an air-blast injection system. After the engine ran for 88 revolutions in February 1894 the Maschinenfabrik Augsburg's stock rose by 30 percent. The engine still caused problems, and Krupp considered cancelling its contract before a third prototype was rushed together.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>In a diesel engine only air, or air mixed with exhaust gas, is compressed in the cylinder. The compression raises its temperature enough to ignite atomised fuel injected at high pressure, so no spark plug is needed. Torque is controlled by changing the amount of fuel injected rather than by throttling the air, and the engine is also called a compression-ignition engine. Its fuel efficiency is better than most other combustion engines because of the high compression ratio and the lack of throttling, reaching up to 43 percent in passenger cars and 55 percent in large two-stroke marine engines. High-speed engines above 1,000 rpm power trucks, buses, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tractor">tractors</a> and cars, while slow-speed engines run below 300 rpm.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In 1893 Diesel suggested that the engine might power lorries, and the first diesel lorries came to market in 1924. The first diesel locomotives appeared in 1913, and diesel engines later replaced steam on all non-electrified railways. Nearly all modern diesel locomotives are diesel–electric, the engine driving a generator that powers electric traction motors. Large ships use slow two-stroke diesels, and submarines are usually diesel–electric.</p><p>Before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> diesel engines also powered aircraft, including the airship Hindenburg. Diesels drive construction and farm machinery and stationary generators. In passenger cars they were long popular in bigger models and have been used in smaller cars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> since the 1980s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Diesel engine” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1893</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Augsburg</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-diesel">Rudolf Diesel</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Critics, among them Otto Köhler and Emil Capitaine, claimed that Diesel had invented nothing new; Capitaine lost a patent lawsuit against him. Diesel may have taken the idea of air-blast injection from George B. Brayton.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/internal-combustion-engine">Internal combustion engine<span>1794</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=diesel-engine">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
